=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6 — the Layer-3 gateway for the 172.18.51.0/24 (switch10/mswitch2) segment where pc15–pc18 reside.

**Mechanism:** Three injected DROP rules on router6's FORWARD chain filter all traffic arriving on eth2 (the backbone interface, 10.50.1.2/24, facing router3): DROP TCP, DROP UDP, and DROP protocol-0 (all-protocols, catching ICMP). Only one narrow ACCEPT precedes them: TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. Because the rules match `in eth2` (inbound from the core), **outbound traffic from pc15–pc18 to the rest of the network is unaffected**, but **all return traffic from the core arriving on eth2 is silently dropped**. ICMP echo-replies, TCP SYN-ACKs, and UDP responses never reach pc15–pc18, producing 100% packet loss (26 packets / 2184 B already dropped by rule 4 in the running counter). The routing plane is intact on all routers: router6 has RIP-learned routes to 172.16.10.0/24, and router0/router3 have kernel static routes back to 172.18.51.0/24.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, rules matching `--in-interface eth2`.

**Root cause:** An iptables ACL injected on router6 blocks all protocol traffic (TCP, UDP, ICMP) returning from the core network through eth2, isolating the entire 172.18.51.x subnet. Router0 and its RIP process are healthy and are not the fault source — the user's reported theory is incorrect.

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain:  
`iptables -D FORWARD -p tcp -i eth2 -j DROP`  
`iptables -D FORWARD -p udp -i eth2 -j DROP`  
`iptables -D FORWARD -p 0 -i eth2 -j DROP`  
(or flush and rebuild with stateful `ESTABLISHED,RELATED` permits if policy enforcement is intended).
=== END ===